  "worked_solution": "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{15}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2+3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x+k) \\\\  & =2(2)+k \\\\  & =4+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(2)=4+k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 4+k & =3 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=2$ for the value $k=-1.$",
